{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:50:14.216596", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glutamate\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.33\n- Feedback: Connected network with a sensible convergence on alpha-ketoglutarate, but it is not a valid synthesis network as written. The deterministic check finds 7 of 7 reactions unbalanced and none fixable by stoichiometric coefficients alone: rxn_001 and rxn_003 need explicit oxidants or electron sinks, rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified and at minimum needs corrected coproducts plus likely intermediate nodes, rxn_006 needs H2O, and rxn_007 needs an explicit photoredox donor rather than bare UV and ZnS. The hydrothermal setting is also asked to support both oxidation and reduction without a stated redox gradient, mixed hydrothermal to surface pathways lack a justified transfer step, and the molecule metadata should mark fumarate, malate, oxaloacetate, and pyruvate as starting nodes if those are intended entry points. P5 is the best current scaffold, but no pathway is approvable until conservation and environment details are corrected.\n- Issues: ['The hydrothermal lineage is conceptually connected, but every step in P1 fails conservation as written: rxn_001 needs an explicit redox partner or H2 product, rxn_002 needs H2O as a reactant, rxn_003 needs an oxidant or H sink, rxn_004 needs CO2 as a product, rxn_005 likely needs H2O as a coproduct and may require intermediate coupling chemistry, and rxn_006 needs H2O as a product. Without those fixes the pathway does not validly reach glutamate.', 'Dropping succinate avoids the weakest dehydrogenation, but P2 still has conservation errors in every reaction: rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_003 needs an oxidant or H sink, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified and missing a coproduct, and rxn_006 needs H2O. The route is more plausible than P1 but still not chemically complete.', 'Starting from malate shortens the path, yet rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, and rxn_006 are all unbalanced. rxn_003 especially needs a specified oxidant, rxn_004 must release CO2, rxn_005 likely needs H2O and intermediate nodes, and rxn_006 needs H2O as coproduct. The backbone logic is coherent, but the chemistry remains under-specified.', 'P4 is one of the more repairable hydrothermal routes because oxaloacetate decarboxylation is chemically plausible, but the written network still violates conservation: rxn_004 must release CO2, rxn_005 needs corrected coupling stoichiometry and likely intermediate chemistry, and rxn_006 needs H2O as product. The target is only reached after those corrections.', 'This is the strongest pathway by brevity, but its two key steps are still invalid as written. rxn_005 is not mass balanced and probably compresses a multi-step pyruvate-glyoxylate network into one node, while rxn_006 also needs H2O as coproduct. If repaired, this would be the clearest route to glutamate.', 'P6 inherits the same mass-balance problems as P1 through rxn_005 and then adds a much weaker terminal step. rxn_007 is especially problematic: alpha-ketoglutarate plus NH3 cannot give glutamate under UV and ZnS without an explicit reductant or electron donor and full proton and water bookkeeping. The hydrothermal-to-surface transfer is also unmodeled.', 'P7 removes rxn_001 but still contains only unbalanced reactions. rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_003 needs an oxidant, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified, and rxn_007 lacks the reductant and coproducts required for a real photoreductive amination. Movement from hydrothermal chemistry to a ZnS surface setting is not justified.', 'The shortened surface route is strategically coherent, but rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, and rxn_007 all remain invalid as written. The final ZnS step is the main blocker because it omits the electrons or sacrificial donor needed for reduction, and the pathway still lacks a transport explanation between environments.', 'P9 is the cleanest of the surface proposals, yet it still depends on three unbalanced steps. rxn_004 needs CO2 release, rxn_005 needs corrected coupling stoichiometry, and rxn_007 is not a credible standalone photoreductive amination without explicit redox accounting. Surface transfer and ZnS availability are also assumed rather than shown.', 'This is the shortest surface route, but it is not chemically defensible in its current form. rxn_005 is oversimplified and unbalanced, while rxn_007 lacks the reductant, protons, and coproduct bookkeeping needed to convert alpha-ketoglutarate and NH3 into glutamate under UV and ZnS. The pyruvate entry point and hydrothermal-to-surface handoff are also only implicit.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Use a convergent alpha-ketoglutarate-centered network in a single surface geothermal setting. Two C2 entry points feed glyoxylate (glycolate oxidation or glyoxal hydration-dehydrogenation), and three C3 entry points feed pyruvate (oxaloacetate decarboxylation, lactate oxidation, or acetaldehyde carboxylation). Glyoxylate and pyruvate then unite in the experimentally motivated pyruvate-glyoxylate condensation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which is converted by a net dehydration-hydrogenation step to alpha-ketoglutarate. Glutamate is reached by two alternative reductive aminations of alpha-ketoglutarate, using either H2 or formic acid/formate as the explicit reductant. Ten representative pathways are enumerated from these combinations.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanedial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxal\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxaloacetic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate dehydrogenation to glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic geothermal mineral-interface pool; Fe2+-rich; mildly acidic to neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-assisted acceptorless dehydrogenation of an alpha-hydroxy acid to the corresponding aldehydo-acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxidation of alpha-hydroxy acids to corresponding oxo or aldehydo acids is a recurring nonenzymatic transformation in iron-mediated prebiotic networks. Glycolate is therefore a chemically reasonable precursor to glyoxylate, and explicit H2 release keeps the redox bookkeeping conservative under anoxic mineral catalysis (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal hydration-dehydrogenation to glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic geothermal pool; hydrated glyoxal adsorbed on sulfide surfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydration of one aldehyde center followed by acceptorless dehydrogenation to the aldehydo-acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glyoxal is a plausible photochemical C2 dialdehyde. Conversion to glyoxylate by hydration plus dehydrogenation is chemically straightforward, although direct prebiotic demonstration under these exact conditions is limited. This route is therefore somewhat speculative, but it is atom-balanced and geochemically compatible with anoxic sulfide-rich surface pools.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxaloacetate decarboxylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic warm pool; oxaloacetate thermally labile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal decarboxylation of a labile oxaloacid to the corresponding alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxaloacetate is intrinsically decarboxylation-prone, and conversion to pyruvate plus CO2 is chemically facile without any need for molecular oxygen or a hidden redox partner. This is one of the cleaner nonenzymatic C4-to-C3 entries in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactate dehydrogenation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic mineral surface; H2 can diffuse away or be consumed downstream\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-assisted dehydrogenation of a hydroxyacid to the corresponding ketoacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lactate and pyruvate form one of the simplest hydroxyacid/ketoacid redox pairs. A metal-mediated dehydrogenation route is therefore chemically conservative and keeps the reducing equivalent explicit as H2 rather than invoking an unstated oxidant (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde carboxylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"CO2-rich, mildly alkaline mineral-interface pool; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization of acetaldehyde followed by carboxylation to the alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Carboxylation of a reactive C2 carbonyl is a chemically economical entry to pyruvate. This step is less directly established than the pyruvate-glyoxylate condensation used later in the network, so it should be regarded as moderately speculative, but acetaldehyde is substantially more carboxylation-prone than acetate and the transformation is fully atom-balanced under CO2-rich mineral-interface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-pyruvate condensation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"concentrated carbonyl pool at Fe2+-rich mineral interface; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+-mediated aldol-type addition of the pyruvate enol/enolate equivalent to glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The pyruvate-glyoxylate union to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate is one of the best supported nonenzymatic entries into alpha-ketoglutarate-like chemistry. It provides a chemically coherent convergence of C2 and C3 feedstocks and is directly aligned with iron-promoted protometabolic networks (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Net dehydration-hydrogenation of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"H2-bearing anoxic mineral interface\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net sequence of beta-hydroxy-ketoacid dehydration to an unsaturated oxodicarboxylate followed by hydrogenation; represented as one balanced node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction is written as the explicit net transformation from 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to alpha-ketoglutarate with H2 as the reductant and H2O as coproduct. Mechanistically it corresponds to a short dehydration-plus-hydrogenation sequence rather than a single elementary event, but collapsing that sequence preserves atom balance while remaining consistent with Fe-mediated pyruvate-glyoxylate networks that access alpha-ketoglutarate (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic NH3-rich pool with intermittent concentration at mineral interfaces\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation between alpha-ketoglutarate and ammonia followed by mineral-catalyzed hydrogenation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alpha-ketoacids readily form imines with ammonia, and reduction of that imine gives the corresponding amino acid. A direct prebiotic demonstration for alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate under exactly these conditions is limited, so this step should be treated as plausible but partly speculative; however, it is fully redox-explicit and chemically conservative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Formate-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic formate-rich mineral-interface pool; intermittent concentration favors imine formation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation followed by transfer hydrogenation from formic acid/formate with decarboxylation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formate is a plausible prebiotic reductant and transfer-hydrogen donor. Using formate instead of H2 supplies an alternative reductive amination route with explicit coproducts CO2 and H2O. This route is more speculative than the H2-driven version, but it is geochemically reasonable in formate-rich surface pools and keeps all redox accounting explicit.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate plus oxaloacetate via H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolate is oxidized to glyoxylate, oxaloacetate decarboxylates to pyruvate, the C2 and C3 units condense to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, and H2 completes reductive amination to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate plus oxaloacetate via formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same convergent carbon-skeleton route as P1, but the terminal amination is driven by formate rather than H2.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate plus lactate via H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Both branch feedstocks are hydroxy acids: glycolate and lactate are oxidized to glyoxylate and pyruvate before converging on alpha-ketoglutarate and glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate plus lactate via formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The hydroxyacid-to-ketoacid branch pair is retained, but the terminal reduction of the alpha-keto acid uses formate as the hydrogen donor.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolate plus acetaldehyde via H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A C2 oxidation route to glyoxylate is paired with aldehyde carboxylation to pyruvate, followed by the common alpha-ketoglutarate and glutamate steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal plus oxaloacetate via H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxal is converted to glyoxylate while oxaloacetate supplies pyruvate; both feed the pyruvate-glyoxylate condensation before H2-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal plus oxaloacetate via formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The glyoxal/oxaloacetate convergence route is retained, but glutamate formation is closed by formate-mediated reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal plus lactate via H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A photochemically plausible C2 dialdehyde entry and a hydroxyacid C3 entry converge through glyoxylate and pyruvate before mineral-mediated amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"