{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T22:06:15.890898", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Glutamate\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.33\n- Feedback: Connected network with a sensible convergence on alpha-ketoglutarate, but it is not a valid synthesis network as written. The deterministic check finds 7 of 7 reactions unbalanced and none fixable by stoichiometric coefficients alone: rxn_001 and rxn_003 need explicit oxidants or electron sinks, rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified and at minimum needs corrected coproducts plus likely intermediate nodes, rxn_006 needs H2O, and rxn_007 needs an explicit photoredox donor rather than bare UV and ZnS. The hydrothermal setting is also asked to support both oxidation and reduction without a stated redox gradient, mixed hydrothermal to surface pathways lack a justified transfer step, and the molecule metadata should mark fumarate, malate, oxaloacetate, and pyruvate as starting nodes if those are intended entry points. P5 is the best current scaffold, but no pathway is approvable until conservation and environment details are corrected.\n- Issues: ['The hydrothermal lineage is conceptually connected, but every step in P1 fails conservation as written: rxn_001 needs an explicit redox partner or H2 product, rxn_002 needs H2O as a reactant, rxn_003 needs an oxidant or H sink, rxn_004 needs CO2 as a product, rxn_005 likely needs H2O as a coproduct and may require intermediate coupling chemistry, and rxn_006 needs H2O as a product. Without those fixes the pathway does not validly reach glutamate.', 'Dropping succinate avoids the weakest dehydrogenation, but P2 still has conservation errors in every reaction: rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_003 needs an oxidant or H sink, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified and missing a coproduct, and rxn_006 needs H2O. The route is more plausible than P1 but still not chemically complete.', 'Starting from malate shortens the path, yet rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, and rxn_006 are all unbalanced. rxn_003 especially needs a specified oxidant, rxn_004 must release CO2, rxn_005 likely needs H2O and intermediate nodes, and rxn_006 needs H2O as coproduct. The backbone logic is coherent, but the chemistry remains under-specified.', 'P4 is one of the more repairable hydrothermal routes because oxaloacetate decarboxylation is chemically plausible, but the written network still violates conservation: rxn_004 must release CO2, rxn_005 needs corrected coupling stoichiometry and likely intermediate chemistry, and rxn_006 needs H2O as product. The target is only reached after those corrections.', 'This is the strongest pathway by brevity, but its two key steps are still invalid as written. rxn_005 is not mass balanced and probably compresses a multi-step pyruvate-glyoxylate network into one node, while rxn_006 also needs H2O as coproduct. If repaired, this would be the clearest route to glutamate.', 'P6 inherits the same mass-balance problems as P1 through rxn_005 and then adds a much weaker terminal step. rxn_007 is especially problematic: alpha-ketoglutarate plus NH3 cannot give glutamate under UV and ZnS without an explicit reductant or electron donor and full proton and water bookkeeping. The hydrothermal-to-surface transfer is also unmodeled.', 'P7 removes rxn_001 but still contains only unbalanced reactions. rxn_002 needs H2O, rxn_003 needs an oxidant, rxn_004 needs CO2, rxn_005 is oversimplified, and rxn_007 lacks the reductant and coproducts required for a real photoreductive amination. Movement from hydrothermal chemistry to a ZnS surface setting is not justified.', 'The shortened surface route is strategically coherent, but rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, and rxn_007 all remain invalid as written. The final ZnS step is the main blocker because it omits the electrons or sacrificial donor needed for reduction, and the pathway still lacks a transport explanation between environments.', 'P9 is the cleanest of the surface proposals, yet it still depends on three unbalanced steps. rxn_004 needs CO2 release, rxn_005 needs corrected coupling stoichiometry, and rxn_007 is not a credible standalone photoreductive amination without explicit redox accounting. Surface transfer and ZnS availability are also assumed rather than shown.', 'This is the shortest surface route, but it is not chemically defensible in its current form. rxn_005 is oversimplified and unbalanced, while rxn_007 lacks the reductant, protons, and coproduct bookkeeping needed to convert alpha-ketoglutarate and NH3 into glutamate under UV and ZnS. The pyruvate entry point and hydrothermal-to-surface handoff are also only implicit.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: The network is structurally valid: it is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and all 9 reactions pass the deterministic atom and charge balance check, so there are no conservation violations. The alpha-ketoglutarate-centered plan is coherent, and rxn_006 is the strongest chemistry because the pyruvate-glyoxylate condensation has good chemical precedent. Confidence is limited by several under-specified transformations, especially acceptorless oxidation to make glyoxylate or pyruvate with H2 release, the direct acetaldehyde plus CO2 to pyruvate step, and the compressed rxn_007 deoxygenation from 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to alpha-ketoglutarate. A single surface geothermal setting could host the network only if local catalytic and redox microenvironments are available. Reductive amination to glutamate is plausible but needs explicit pH and mineral context, and reaction-specific literature support would strengthen the case. P1 is the best-supported pathway, but the network remains below the 0.9 approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['Downstream logic is sound once pyruvate and glyoxylate are formed, but replacing oxaloacetate with lactate lowers confidence because lactate oxidation to pyruvate with H2 release is less secure under generic surface conditions. H2-driven amination is plausible if H2-activating minerals are present.', 'This route is coherent but stacks three medium-confidence steps: glycolate oxidation, lactate oxidation, and formate-driven reductive amination. The cumulative uncertainty makes it weaker than the oxaloacetate-based options.', 'The central condensation chemistry is still reasonable, but the branch to pyruvate is weak because direct acetaldehyde carboxylation to pyruvate is mechanistically under-specified for a generic geothermal surface. That branch becomes the limiting step for the pathway.', 'Chemically coherent, but both the glyoxal entry step and the formate-driven amination need tighter condition control than is currently stated. The route is plausible but less secure than P6 or P1.', 'This pathway combines two moderate-risk oxidation steps before entering the stronger core condensation chemistry. It remains connected and balanced, but the cumulative need for selective dehydrogenation on a single surface setting lowers confidence.', 'The route is logically complete, but it accumulates uncertainty at the entry stage and again in the final formate-driven amination. It is viable only if very specific catalytic and pH conditions are supplied.', 'Lowest-confidence pathway. It uses the weakest pyruvate entry step, a reactive glyoxal entry, and a condition-sensitive formate amination, so too many key transformations are under-specified even though the network remains formally balanced and connected.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"A single anoxic surface-geothermal setting is used throughout, so no hydrothermal-to-surface transfer step is needed. The network converges on alpha-ketoglutarate by two chemically distinct lineages: a C6 tricarboxylate lineage (citrate -> cis-aconitate -> isocitrate -> oxalosuccinate -> alpha-ketoglutarate) and a C2+C3 condensation lineage (pyruvate + glyoxylate -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate -> 2-oxopent-3-enedioate -> alpha-ketoglutarate). Glutamate is then obtained by two explicit reductive amination chemistries, one H2-driven and one formate-driven. Ten pathways are generated by pairing five entry pools with these two terminal amination options, while keeping all redox and water bookkeeping explicit.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopent-3-enedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-oxopent-3-enedioate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8O7\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"citrate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cis-aconitic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cis-aconitate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8O7\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isocitrate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H6O7\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxalosuccinic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxalosuccinate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Citrate dehydration to cis-aconitate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\", \"silica surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+ on silica-rich mineral surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, UV-shielded geothermal film; partial drying favors dehydration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-assisted elimination of water from citrate to form the unsaturated tricarboxylate cis-aconitate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Citrate dehydration is one of the simpler non-enzymatic TCA-like interconversions under hot, mineral-rich conditions. Writing this as a separate node keeps water bookkeeping explicit and avoids hiding dehydration inside a later rearrangement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"cis-Aconitate hydration to isocitrate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\", \"silica surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+ on silica-rich mineral surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic rewetting phase in the same geothermal setting\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-surface hydration of the C=C bond of cis-aconitate to regenerate a C6 tricarboxylate alcohol, tracked here as isocitrate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aconitate can rehydrate non-enzymatically; the key limitation is regioselectivity because citrate is a plausible competing product. This branch is retained because some isocitrate formation is chemically reasonable in a mineral-controlled setting and it provides a transparent route to oxalosuccinate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate dehydrogenation to oxalosuccinate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiFe sulfide\", \"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiFe sulfide and magnetite surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic mineral film or pore water; evolved H2 can escape locally\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated alcohol-to-ketone dehydrogenation of isocitrate, with H2 as the explicit electron sink, giving oxalosuccinate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step makes the redox partner explicit rather than leaving oxidation unspecified. Dehydrogenation on H2-activating metal or sulfide surfaces is chemically plausible, though less secure than simple hydration or dehydration steps; it is included because oxalosuccinate is the correct beta-keto precursor to alpha-ketoglutarate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalosuccinate decarboxylation to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"warm anoxic geothermal pore water; decarboxylation aided by heating\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thermal decarboxylation of a beta-keto tricarboxylic acid equivalent to yield the C5 alpha-keto diacid alpha-ketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Once oxalosuccinate is present, decarboxylation to alpha-ketoglutarate is the strongest step in the C6 lineage. The beta-ketoacid-like motif makes CO2 loss mechanistically straightforward and thermodynamically favorable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol addition to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"dissolved Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, Fe2+-rich geothermal water; mineral films provide UV shielding\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+-promoted aldol-type C-C bond formation between pyruvate and glyoxylate to give a C5 beta-hydroxy alpha-keto diacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is among the better-supported prebiotic entries into metabolism-like C5 chemistry. Making 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate explicit avoids the earlier over-compressed pyruvate-plus-glyoxylate-to-alpha-ketoglutarate shortcut and keeps atom balance transparent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"4-Hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate dehydration to 2-oxopent-3-enedioate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica surface\", \"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica-rich mineral surfaces with Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"partial drying on hot mineral surfaces under anoxic conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydration of the beta-hydroxy alpha-keto diacid to an unsaturated alpha-keto diacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Beta-hydroxy keto diacids should dehydrate more readily than they should undergo direct one-step deoxygenation. This explicit dehydration step is included to avoid hiding the water loss and to make the downstream hydrogenation chemically more credible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"2-Oxopent-3-enedioate hydrogenation to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiFe sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiFe sulfide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal gas input over metal-sulfide grains\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-catalyzed hydrogenation of the C=C bond in the unsaturated alpha-keto diacid to produce alpha-ketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrogenation of an alkene on H2-activating sulfide surfaces is chemically cleaner than invoking an unspecified deoxygenation. This step provides explicit redox bookkeeping for the pyruvate-glyoxylate branch, though it remains less directly demonstrated than the initial aldol addition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiFe sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiFe sulfide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"anoxic NH3-bearing microzones with H2-rich volcanic or geothermal gas contact\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with the alpha-keto group to form an imine/iminium equivalent, followed by surface hydrogenation to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Using H2 explicitly fixes the missing-reductant problem that often makes prebiotic amination schemes under-specified. In a Ni/Fe-sulfide-rich geothermal setting, imine formation plus hydrogenation is chemically plausible and gives a direct route from alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Formate-driven reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica surface\", \"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"silica or clay mineral surface with Fe2+ present\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"formate concentrated by evaporation-rewetting in anoxic geothermal pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation between alpha-ketoglutarate and ammonia followed by transfer hydrogenation from formate, with decarboxylation of formate to CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formate provides a second explicit reductive amination chemistry that does not depend on dissolved H2 in the terminal step. This parallel branch is chemically reasonable in formate-bearing geothermal pools and broadens the network without introducing hidden redox assumptions.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Citrate route with H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry from citrate through dehydration, rehydration, oxidative decarboxylation, then H2-driven reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Citrate route with formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Same C6 lineage as P1, but the terminal amination uses formate as the reductant.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"cis-Aconitate route with H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry from cis-aconitate, followed by hydration to isocitrate, oxidation to oxalosuccinate, decarboxylation, and H2-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"cis-Aconitate route with formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Downstream C6 subpath from cis-aconitate, ending with formate-driven reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate route with H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Shortest complete C6 oxidation-decarboxylation route from isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate, then H2-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Isocitrate route with formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Isocitrate oxidation and decarboxylation followed by formate-driven reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalosuccinate route with H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct beta-ketoacid decarboxylation entry from oxalosuccinate, then H2-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalosuccinate route with formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct beta-ketoacid decarboxylation entry from oxalosuccinate, then formate-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate route with H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A C2+C3 condensation branch: aldol addition, dehydration, hydrogenation to alpha-ketoglutarate, then H2-driven amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate-glyoxylate route with formate amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The pyruvate-glyoxylate branch paired with formate-driven terminal amination to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_007\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_007\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate", "overall_strategy": "A single anoxic surface-geothermal setting is used throughout, so no hydrothermal-to-surface transfer step is needed. The network converges on alpha-ketoglutarate by two chemically distinct lineages: a C6 tricarboxylate lineage (citrate -> cis-aconitate -> isocitrate -> oxalosuccinate -> alpha-ketoglutarate) and a C2+C3 condensation lineage (pyruvate + glyoxylate -> 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate -> 2-oxopent-3-enedioate -> alpha-ketoglutarate). Glutamate is then obtained by two explicit reductive amination chemistries, one H2-driven and one formate-driven. Ten pathways are generated by pairing five entry pools with these two terminal amination options, while keeping all redox and water bookkeeping explicit.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CO2", "iupac